# =============================================================================
# GOVERNANCE RULES AND GUIDELINES FOR AI AGENTS — TREEGOV
# Project: TreeGov — Risk Segmentation Workbench
# =============================================================================

## MANDATORY when starting or resuming a session

1. Read README.md in full.
2. Read docs/methodology.md, docs/audit_log.md, and the philosophy document (docs/philosophy.md in English, docs/philosophy.es.md in Spanish).
3. Read this file (.treegovrules) in full.
4. Read AGENTS.md in full to check the current reasoning-level policy and burst-mode rule.

## White-box rules (HARD RESTRICTION)

1. FORBIDDEN to use: sklearn, scikit-learn, statsmodels, xgboost, lightgbm, catboost.
2. Only allowed: numpy, pandas, scipy.stats.
3. Every statistical formula must be written explicitly in the code, with its bibliographic citation in the docstring.

## Clean-room architecture

1. backend_v3/ MUST NOT import PySide6 or anything from frontend_v3/.
2. frontend_v3/ MUST NOT implement analytical logic.
3. SplitConfig is the common contract between engines. Its immutability (frozen dataclass) is sacred.

## MRM / IFRS 9 governance

1. In Banking mode (default), any PD calculation must include the Jeffreys estimator and the PD Floor (BCBS CRE 20.39). In Research mode, PD is raw (k/n), with no Bayesian smoothing and no floor — see backend_v3.types.GovernanceMode. The choice of mode is explicit and is recorded in the model's params_fingerprint (BANKING / RESEARCH / CUSTOM). A model trained under one mode must never be silently reused under another.
2. Every model decision must be auditable: transactional DEBUG log per run.
3. Tests must include reference values verified by hand (not just self-references).

## Protected zones (NEVER touch without explicit authorization)

- .venv/
- data/
- archive/
- logs/ (write-access only via log_config.py)

## Mandatory documentation

1. Every architectural decision is recorded in docs/audit_log.md.
2. Every bug fix is recorded with its numerical trace.
3. Obsolete documents are moved to archive/legacy_docs/ with a historical header, never deleted.
4. Phase closure documentation is never deferred. When a sub-phase
   closes (commit pushed, suite green), the update of
   docs/audit_log.md, docs/roadmap.md, docs/estado_proyecto.md,
   docs/contexto_para_nueva_sesion.md, docs/prompt_para_asistente.md,
   the README pair (README.md / README.es.md), and docs/api.md
   happens IN THE SAME work block, never in a later session.
   Exceptions: provider outage (504/timeout, documented on return)
   or a one-line trivial change consolidated into the block entry.
5. **Audit consumers before changing public function signatures.** When a function gains mandatory parameters, every consumer of that function must be found and adapted in the SAME work block. Not in the next sub-phase. Rationale: the 15 avoidable failures of Phase 3.3.d came from not auditing the `_cfg()` helpers of the E2E tests before changing `_train_pipeline`.
6. **PyPI release rule.** PyPI freezes the README at upload
   time. If the README of the repository changes after a
   release, the public page stays out of sync until the next
   release. When modifying the README, decide explicitly: (a)
   publish a new version immediately, or (b) wait for the next
   release with code changes. Never leave the decision implicit.
   This rule was materialised on 12/10/2026: 1.0.0a3 was
   published with the README in its pre-synchronization state;
   the three subsequent README commits are visible on GitHub but
   not on PyPI. Resolved by publishing 1.0.0a4.

Synchronization checklist (mandatory at every sub-phase closure,
expanded to 6 documents on 12/10/2026): (a) docs/roadmap.md
reflects the new status; (b) docs/estado_proyecto.md reflects
the real test count; (c) docs/contexto_para_nueva_sesion.md
reflects the next step; (d) docs/prompt_para_asistente.md
reflects the active tool and current phase; (e) README.md /
README.es.md reflect the current version, the phase table
status, and the test suite count; (f) docs/api.md reflects the
current public API surface and does not carry stale statements
about blocking behaviour. Any sub-phase whose closure leaves any
of the six out of sync is not considered closed. Motive: three
documentation gaps were detected on 12/10/2026 (repository
structure in READMEs, per-file test counts in estado_proyecto
§7, PyPI version in estado_proyecto §1) that the previous
four-document checklist did not cover.

## Language

- Code: English.
- Documentation: English for technical and reference documents (README, docs/methodology.md, docs/design_decisions.md, docs/api.md, docs/roadmap.md, docs/3.x_design_*.md). Spanish for operational and traceability documents (docs/audit_log.md, docs/estado_proyecto.md, docs/contexto_para_nueva_sesion.md, docs/prompt_para_asistente.md). Bilingual: README.md / README.es.md and docs/philosophy.md / docs/philosophy.es.md.
- Bilingual documents: README and philosophy (both English and Spanish).
- Historical document: audit_log.md (kept in Spanish for traceability).
- File and module names: English.

## Anti-leakage

Any imputation (mean/mode) is computed EXCLUSIVELY on the training fold. TrainOnlyImputer is the only authorized channel.

## Prohibitions in public documentation

- Do not mention third-party trademarks nor the names of the predecessor project.
- Do mention: academic citations (Kass, Breiman, etc.), regulatory standards (Basel, IFRS 9, SR 11-7).

## Verification

Before modifying existing code: read README.md, docs/methodology.md and this file.
